1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,089
Book Four, Proposition One.

2
00:00:02,089 --> 00:00:09,195
Into a given circle to fit a segment equal to a given segment which is not greater than the diameter of the circle.

3
00:00:09,195 --> 00:00:20,010
Let A B C be the given circle, and D the given segment not greater than the diameter of the circle; it is required to fit into the circle A B C a chord equal to the segment D.

4
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:23,688
Draw a diameter B C of the circle A B C.

5
00:00:23,688 --> 00:00:28,169
If B C equals D, that which was enjoined has been done.

6
00:00:28,169 --> 00:00:33,545
But if B C is greater than D, cut off C E equal to D by One Three.

7
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:43,320
With center C and defining radius point E, describe the circle E A F, cutting the circle A B C at A and F, and join C A.

8
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:52,217
Because the point C is the center of the circle E A F, the radius C A equals the radius C E by Definition Fifteen.

9
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:58,611
Yet C E was constructed equal to D; therefore by Common Notion One, C A also equals D.

10
00:00:58,611 --> 00:01:05,898
Thus into the given circle A B C there has been fitted the chord C A equal to the given segment D.

11
00:01:05,898 --> 00:01:07,460
Which was to be done.

12
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:14,334
Proposition Four One launches Book Four's study of figures inscribed in and circumscribed about circles.

13
00:01:14,334 --> 00:01:34,937
From any chosen point C on the circumference, the auxiliary circle E A F yields two symmetric chords C A and C F, while varying C around the circle generates a family of equal chords tangent to a concentric inner circle—the basis of Pappus's parallel-chord construction and Apollonius's celebrated neusis, or verging-chord, problem.

14
00:01:34,937 --> 00:01:53,120
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred forty-seven joins circle radii and diameter properties with Definition Four Seven, becoming a foundational tool for ninety-nine downstream propositions across Books Four, Ten, Eleven, and Thirteen, including the golden triangle and regular pentagon.

15
00:01:53,540 --> 00:02:07,545
